Research Direction II: Learning NIR-to-RGB Translation with Limited and Unreliable RGB Supervision
Proposed Paper Title: Learning Visible Reconstruction from NIR with Scarce and Degraded RGB Supervision
3.1 Research Gap
A major difficulty in NIR-to-RGB translation is the dependence on paired NIR and RGB training images.
The problem becomes more challenging under adverse conditions because the corresponding RGB image may itself contain severe noise, blur, saturation, low visibility, or other degradations.
For example, if we train a model using a clear NIR image paired with a severely dark RGB image, the conventional reconstruction objective encourages the model to reproduce the degraded RGB observation.
This may not be the intended objective if we want to recover a more useful visible appearance.
However, we must distinguish reconstructing the simultaneously observed RGB image from estimating a clean or better-exposed visible reference. These are different targets and cannot be treated as interchangeable.
The central research question is:
How can we learn useful visible reconstruction from NIR when reliable paired RGB training images are limited, degraded, or unavailable?
This problem is relevant to nighttime imaging, adverse-weather capture, and other situations where collecting dependable paired RGB supervision is difficult.
3.2 Research Objective
We would investigate a learning framework capable of using training data with different supervision quality.
The available data may be represented as:
[
\mathcal{D}=
\mathcal{D}{\mathrm{reliable}}
\cup
\mathcal{D}{\mathrm{degraded}}
\cup
\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{NIR-only}}
]
where:
(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{reliable}}) contains paired NIR–RGB images with trustworthy visible references.
(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{degraded}}) contains paired observations affected by measurable RGB degradation.
(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{NIR-only}}) contains NIR images without paired RGB supervision.
The main research challenge is determining how these different data sources should contribute to learning without introducing incorrect color or structural information.
Simply combining supervised and unsupervised losses would not be sufficient novelty. We would need a principled learning mechanism and evidence that it outperforms standard semi-supervised, unpaired and pseudo-labeling approaches.
