3.4 Experiments
We would train models using progressively smaller proportions of reliable paired supervision, such as:
1%, 5%, 10%, 25%, and 100%.
These are proposed experimental settings.
We would also investigate how different types and severities of RGB-reference degradation affect reconstruction learning.
The method would be compared against fully supervised training, limited-pair supervised training, CoColor, unpaired translation, conventional self-training, and appropriate pseudo-pairing baselines.
Performance should be evaluated using trustworthy visible reference images, rather than treating synthetic or hypothetical clear-weather RGB images as automatically correct ground truth.
3.5 Expected Research Contribution
The intended contribution is a new learning approach that remains effective when reliable RGB supervision is scarce.
The research would be particularly meaningful if the method improves reconstruction without requiring large amounts of accurately paired RGB training data.
Potential publication scope: IEEE TIP, IEEE TCI, CVPR, ICCV, or another suitable high-quality journal/conference.
Major risk: Unpaired image translation and limited-data colorization already exist. We would need to demonstrate that the proposed method solves a meaningful supervision-quality limitation that conventional approaches do not adequately handle.
4. Research Direction III: Spectral Ambiguity and Reliable NIR-to-RGB Reconstruction
Proposed Paper Title: Identifiability-Aware NIR-to-Visible Reconstruction under Spectral Ambiguity
4.1 Research Gap
One fundamental limitation of NIR-to-RGB translation is that a single NIR image does not always contain enough information to determine the correct visible colors.
Two materials can exhibit similar NIR reflectance while having significantly different visible appearances.
Consequently, even a powerful reconstruction network may produce a plausible RGB image without being able to determine whether its predicted colors are correct.
This is not simply an architectural limitation; it is related to the information available in the spectral measurement itself.
The main research question is:
Can we identify situations where visible appearance is inherently ambiguous from NIR measurements and develop reconstruction models that represent this ambiguity reliably?
4.2 Research Objective
We would first characterize ambiguity using measured spectral data.
Let (F_N(\rho)) represent the NIR sensor response for a material spectrum (\rho).
Two materials may satisfy:
[
|F_N(\rho_a)-F_N(\rho_b)|\leq\epsilon
]
while their visible appearances differ significantly:
[
\Delta E_{00}(C_a,C_b)>\tau
]
This means that the sensor cannot reliably distinguish those materials under the specified measurement and noise conditions, even though their visible appearances are different.
We would investigate how to characterize such cases and develop a reconstruction framework that can express uncertainty or multiple valid color hypotheses.
The research must go beyond generating several visually plausible RGB images. We need to evaluate whether the predicted uncertainty corresponds to physically supported ambiguity.
