The complete ICVL spectral data are important because the commonly used 31-band visible subset does not contain 850 nm and 940 nm NIR measurements.
4.4 Experiments
We would investigate:
Material pairs with similar NIR responses and different visible appearances.
Reconstruction errors associated with spectral ambiguity.
Whether deterministic models become confidently incorrect in ambiguous cases.
Whether probabilistic reconstruction models generate informative alternatives.
Whether the proposed method gives better uncertainty calibration and more useful prediction sets than existing approaches.
Possible evaluation metrics include color error, sample-based probabilistic scores, empirical coverage, prediction-set size, and performance on held-out materials and sensors.
4.5 Expected Research Contribution
The intended contribution is a scientifically grounded approach to evaluating and representing the limits of NIR-based visible reconstruction.
This direction could be important because conventional reconstruction benchmarks generally compare a prediction against one RGB target, even when the underlying inverse problem admits multiple plausible outcomes.
However, spectral metamerism, diffusion-based colorization and conformal uncertainty estimation are already established research areas.
Therefore, the novelty must come from a demonstrably new inference or evaluation principle, not merely adding an uncertainty map.
Potential publication scope: CVPR, ICCV, IEEE TCI, IEEE TIP, or computational imaging venues.
Major risk: Existing generative reconstruction models combined with standard calibration may already provide comparable results. This needs to be tested before we finalize any architecture.
